<h1>Package management reinvented</h1>

<p>
  All the package managers are the same nowadays. <br>
  Installing and publishing packages is painful, and nobody's improved it yet. <br>
  Between Yarn, RubyGems, PIP, npm, it's all the same painful process. <br>

  <br>

  With Cranium, I put my foot down; this sense of complacency with packages has to end; <br>
  Packages deserve as much love as the rest of your stack, <br>
  and given that packages are so prevalent in the modern day world,
  why do we treat PMs like an afterthought? <br>

  <br>

  Now, of course, I don't want to bikeshed packages too much; I'll admit that package managers aren't entirely wrong. <br>
  However, they are way too painful. They do the right things, but they do those things the wrong way; they do the right things the wrong way. <br>
  Cranium does those same things the right (painless) way. <br>

  <br>

  Because of this, Cranium isn't an entirely new tool you have to learn. In fact, I designed Cranium to be extremely easy to learn, <br>
  and I designed it with the principle of least surprise in mind—
  you can install a package with get or fetch alike, along with plenty more aliases! <br>

  <br>

  I also designed Cranium to State The Obvious — my personal philosophy. <br>
  What might a person want to do on a package manager's website? The answer is almost certainly one of two things: <br>
  they want to either find/install a package, or upload a package. <br>

  <br>

  So why does Yarn, as well as plenty of other PMs, make that process unbearably painful? <br>
  It's the equivalent of walking into a bar, ordering a beer, and then being met with a confused gaze by the bartender. <br>
  Cranium will never meet you with a confused gaze, as long as you are sensible and order a beer, and not a bowl of jelly beans.
</p>

